  <h2 id="sales">Who pays for these skills, and how they judge you</h2>

  <p>
    Employers and clients pay for working skill. They judge you by projects, tests and clear explanations.
  </p>
  <p>
    The first basic skill is a project report with a problem, method and result.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ai">AI and add-on courses for engineering students: which tasks are under pressure</h2>

  <p>
    AI tools write code and summaries. Basic output from add-on courses becomes common.
  </p>
  <p>
    Problem selection, testing and responsibility hold value. A staged path helps: now, use AI to learn faster; later, own design choices and checks.
  </p>

  <h2 id="better">Alternatives that can beat a general add-on courses for engineering students path</h2>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Alternative</th>
          <th>Why it can beat it</th>
          <th>Catch</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Alternative"><strong>Higher study</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why it can beat it">Deeper skill</td>
          <td data-label="Catch">Costs time and money</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Alternative"><strong>Government exam route</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why it can beat it">Stable path</td>
          <td data-label="Catch">Needs long preparation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Alternative"><strong>Startup route</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why it can beat it">Fast learning</td>
          <td data-label="Catch">Uncertain pay</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="learn">How to learn add-on courses for engineering students cheaply, and what proof counts</h2>

  <p>
    Add-on courses for engineering students is learned by doing real work, so the cheapest route is also the best. A paid course is worth it only when it gives feedback and finished work that you cannot get free.
  </p>
  <h3>Free and low-cost routes first</h3>
  <ul class="check-list">
    <li>Free NPTEL and SWAYAM courses.</li>
    <li>Free YouTube lessons and open documentation.</li>
    <li>Free tools and community forums.</li>
    <li>A senior or faculty member who reviews your project.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>How to judge a paid course</h3>
  <p>
    Judge each course, not the platform. Ask what you will build, who reviews it, which role graduates reached, how current the material is and what the refund terms are, in writing.
  </p>
  <h3>Your week, and a first small test</h3>
  <p>
    A starting plan is about ten hours a week: three of lessons and seven of practice on one project. Do not pay for an expensive programme before this habit holds.
  </p>
  <p>
    In four weeks, finish one small project in your add-on skill and ask a faculty member for one correction.
  </p>
  <h3>Building a high-income skill portfolio beside the credential</h3>
  <p>
    A degree has a place, and the weak spot is relying on it alone. A skill mix of one core skill, a second skill, communication, a niche and a clear offer builds a high-income skill portfolio, and a niche service can bring an earlier financial freedom.
  </p>

  <h2 id="plan">A semester-by-semester add-on plan</h2>

  <p>
    Pick skills by the notices you plan to apply to. The branch matters less than the proof.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Stage</th>
          <th>Add-on focus</th>
          <th>Proof to build</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Stage"><strong>Year 1 to 2</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Add-on focus">Programming basics, spreadsheets, writing</td>
          <td data-label="Proof to build">Small tools and a notes folder</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Stage"><strong>Year 2 to 3</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Add-on focus">One domain skill that fits your branch</td>
          <td data-label="Proof to build">A project with a short report</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Stage"><strong>Year 3 to 4</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Add-on focus">Interview and communication practice</td>
          <td data-label="Proof to build">A mock interview recording</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Stage"><strong>Final year</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Add-on focus">One capstone linking skill and domain</td>
          <td data-label="Proof to build">A project with test results</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Review the plan each semester against ten fresh job notices.
  </p>

  <h2 id="branches">What to add by branch family</h2>

  <p>
    These are starting ideas. Always compare them with notices for your target roles.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Branch family</th>
          <th>Add-on to consider</th>
          <th>Reason</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Branch family"><strong>Computer and IT</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Add-on to consider">Data structures practice, cloud basics, SQL</td>
          <td data-label="Reason">Common in screening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Branch family"><strong>Electronics and electrical</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Add-on to consider">Embedded basics, PCB basics, simulation tools</td>
          <td data-label="Reason">Hands-on work is judged</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Branch family"><strong>Mechanical and civil</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Add-on to consider">Design software, project planning, quality basics</td>
          <td data-label="Reason">Tools and planning appear in notices</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Branch family"><strong>Any branch</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Add-on to consider">Data skills, clear writing, presentation</td>
          <td data-label="Reason">Used across roles</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="money">Money: course fees and the 10 percent test</h2>

  <p>
    As a rule of thumb and not a research finding, keep a degree or course fee near a tenth of the family's total education budget, and treat spending above roughly a fifth as a serious caution that needs course-specific evidence.
  </p>
  <p>
    An illustration, not a real fee: on a Rs 8 lakh education budget, a tenth is Rs 80,000 for the main course, and a fifth is Rs 1.6 lakh as a serious caution. The rest can fund a laptop, practice time and a reserve.
  </p>
  <p>
    Test the field with free courses and one finished project before paying for a long programme. Do not borrow heavily for a course whose graduates cannot show placement in a specific role.
  </p>

  <h2 id="checkpoint">The 4-Checkpoint Protocol for this career</h2>

  <p>
    Test the choice with <strong>The 4-Checkpoint Protocol</strong>: Biology, Context, Market and Survival.
  </p>
  <div class="protocol">
    <div class="protocol-row">
      <div class="protocol-row__num">01</div>
      <div>
        <div class="protocol-row__title">Biology: which kind of work holds your attention?</div>
        <p>Try one small build in two areas.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="protocol-row">
      <div class="protocol-row__num">02</div>
      <div>
        <div class="protocol-row__title">Context: time, money and home support</div>
        <p>Count study time and costs.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="protocol-row">
      <div class="protocol-row__num">03</div>
      <div>
        <div class="protocol-row__title">Market: who hires?</div>
        <p>Read ten notices for your branch.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="protocol-row">
      <div class="protocol-row__num">04</div>
      <div>
        <div class="protocol-row__title">Survival: what survives AI?</div>
        <p>Routine output shrinks. Judgment holds value.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

